WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.939
Mind your decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:11.429
Three runners X, Y and Z compete in a 1000-meters race. Each runs with a different uniform speed.

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:18.959
If X faces Y, X gives Y a start of 50 meters so they complete the race at the same time.

00:00:19.779 --> 00:00:25.739
If X faces Z, X gives Z a 69-meters start so they complete the race at the same time.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.979
The question is, "If Y faces Z,

00:00:29.199 --> 00:00:34.289
"...how much of a start does Y have to give Z so they complete the race at the same time?"

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:38.009
This is adapted from India's Civil Services Exam.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:45.400
Pause the video if you'd like to give it a try, and when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem.

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.340
Now, many people would think like this:

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.509
X gives Y a start of 50 meters and X gives Z a start of 69 meters.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:09.739
So comparing Y and Z we have a difference of 19 meters.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.700
This is close but not the correct answer.

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.640
The reason is that Y is only running 950 meters.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:22.250
We need the answer for a 1,000-meter race. So let's go through the calculation.

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:29.359
We'll use the formula that distance is equal to rate times time, which is often abbreviated "d is equal to r t" (d = rt).

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:37.240
Now X is running 1,000 meters in the same time that Y is running 950 meters.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.700
Consequently, we can set up the following equations

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:45.200
1,000 is equal to X speed multiplied by the time and

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:50.060
950 is equal to Y speed multiplied by the same time to complete the race.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:57.709
We'll solve both of these equations for t. And since they're both equal to the same t, we'll set them equal to each other.

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:02.959
We'll then solve for y and get y is equal to 0.95x.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:07.009
We'll set that aside and we'll do the same calculation for z.

00:02:07.739 --> 00:02:10.399
Z has to run 931 meters.

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:16.219
Now we could just go ahead and say that z is equal to 0.931x.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:21.110
But we'll go ahead and do this the slow way. We'll solve for t in each of these equations,

00:02:21.629 --> 00:02:25.009
we'll set them equal to each other and then we'll solve for z.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.640
We get z is equal to 0.931x.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.780
We have two equations and we'll divide the second from the first.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.819
The x terms cancel out and then this fraction simplifies to be .98.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:44.149
It'll be convenient to write it as 98 over 100, which is equal to 980 over 1000.

00:02:44.519 --> 00:02:49.789
We'll then exchange the 980 and the y to get y over 1000 is equal to z over 980.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:55.099
This means Y runs 1,000 meters in the same time Z runs 980 meters.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:03.229
1000 minus 980 is equal to 20 and thus we get the answer that Y has to give a 20 meter start to Z.

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:08.460
I think this is a great problem because it has a slightly counterintuitive answer.

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:12.980
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:18.460
As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

